```cpp
uint32_t hash () const
{
    return hb_bytes_t (head, tail - head).hash () ^
         links.as_bytes ().hash ();
}
```

This hashes the bytes from `head` to `tail`. After `pop_pack()`:
```cpp
obj->tail = head;  // serialize head at this point
...
unsigned len = obj->tail - obj->head;
head = obj->head; /* Rewind head. */
...
tail -= len;
memmove (tail, obj->head, len);
obj->head = tail;
obj->tail = tail + len;
```

So the object's content is moved to the tail region of the buffer. The content itself is what was serialized, and serialization uses `allocate_size` which zeros memory.

